---
<LandingLayout
  title="How AI agents prove who they are, and what they may do — FORA resources"
  description="Web Bot Auth, OAuth and MCP, card networks, sign-in providers and anonymous credentials: what each one proves about an AI agent, and where a publisher meets it."
>
  <AudienceHero
    current="resources"
    badge={`Resources · Identity · ${a(WBA, 'Web Bot Auth')}`}
    title="How AI agents prove who they are, <em>and what they may do.</em>"
    sub="When an AI agent asks for your content, four questions matter: who it is, whom it acts for, what it may do, and who pays. No one standard answers all four. This page shows which one answers which, and where you'll meet each."
    updated={resourceUpdated(HREF)}
  >
    <a href="#map" class="btn btn-primary">The four questions</a>
    <a href="#need" class="btn btn-ghost">What you need</a>
  </AudienceHero>

  <Band tone="near" id="map" label="The four questions" title="Four questions, and what answers each">
    <Split aside="380px">
      <ResourceTable label="Four questions about an AI agent, and what answers each" cols={['', 'What answers it', 'Where you meet it']} rows={QUESTIONS} labelAll />
      <Figure
        slot="aside"
        src="/resources/web-bot-auth/wba-5.png"
        title="The identity layers."
        alt="Five layers of agent identity, bottom to top. Network hints: user agents, IP lists and reverse DNS, drawn dashed. Which domain's key signed it: Web Bot Auth, drawn heaviest, with Client ID Metadata Document, and also mTLS and AAuth. Whom it acts for, and whether a real person stands behind it: Sign in with ChatGPT, World ID. What it may do: MCP, Okta Cross App Access, Entra Agent ID. Who pays: Visa Trusted Agent Protocol and Mastercard Agent Pay, on the same signatures as Web Bot Auth. Anonymous credentials sit beside the identity and real-person layers."
      />
    </Split>
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="need" label="What you need" title="What you need depends on what you sell">
    <PointList tone="neutral" items={NEED} />
    <p class="prose">The rest of this page you can leave to your engineers for now.</p>
  </Band>

  <Band id="token" label="Signature and token" title="How a signature differs from an OAuth token">
    <Split aside="380px">
      <div>
        <p class="prose" set:html={h('The agent signs each Web Bot Auth request itself, and the signature says which website it belongs to, nothing more [1]. An OAuth access token comes from a sign-in server and lists the scopes, what the agent may do. In its JWT form it also names the client, and the server signs it [8, 17].')} />
        <p class="prose" set:html={h('A plain token is a bearer token: whoever holds it can use it [18]. OAuth\'s security best practice asks servers to bind tokens to the client\'s key, with mutual TLS or DPoP [19].')} />
      </div>
      <Figure
        slot="aside"
        src="/resources/web-bot-auth/wba-9.png"
        title="A signature and a token."
        alt="Two things side by side. Web Bot Auth: a request with the agent's own signature on it, which tells you who is asking. An OAuth token, a JWT: a card that says who the agent is, what it may do (read articles) and until when (one hour), signed by the issuer, which tells you who is asking and what it may do."
      />
    </Split>
    <ResourceTable label="An API key, an OAuth token and a Web Bot Auth signature compared" cols={['', 'API key', 'OAuth token', 'Web Bot Auth signature']} rows={CREDENTIALS} labelAll />
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('An agent can carry both: a signature for who it is and a token for what it may do. FORA\'s own signature covers the request\'s token header too [12]. Permissions can also shrink as they pass along: whoever holds a Biscuit token can add restrictions without asking the issuer, and the token "will never gain more rights" [23].')} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" id="oauth" label="OAuth and MCP" title="How an agent gets permission through OAuth and MCP">
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('If you want subscribers\' AI assistants to read what they\'ve paid for, OAuth is how. A reader signs in through your login and approves what the assistant may do, and your server lets the assistant in for that reader only. MCP, the protocol AI assistants use to reach tools and data, uses OAuth when a server requires permission [7]:')} />
    <Split aside="420px">
      <StepList items={MCP_STEPS} />
      <Figure
        slot="aside"
        src={`${IMG}/id-1.png`}
        title="Permission through MCP."
        alt="How an agent gets permission through MCP, across the reader, the agent, your MCP server and the sign-in server. The agent calls your MCP server with no token and gets a 401 that says where to get one. The agent asks the sign-in server for permission to read articles; the reader signs in and approves. The sign-in server gives the agent a token for reading articles, and the agent calls your MCP server again with the token, which your server accepts."
      />
    </Split>
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('MCP prefers that the app\'s ID be a web address pointing to a document the app publishes about itself, much like a Web Bot Auth key directory [21]. An agent that buys for its own company, with no reader involved, needs none of this flow: Web Bot Auth and a license can be enough.')} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="payments" label="Card networks" title="When agents pay by card">
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('Visa and Mastercard both use the same kind of signature as Web Bot Auth. Visa\'s Trusted Agent Protocol builds on the same internet standard and is aligned with Web Bot Auth. An agent signs both while it browses and when it pays, and a valid signature "proves the Agent\'s participation in the Payment Scheme specific program" [15]. Mastercard Agent Pay certifies agents and lists them in a registry that "Your systems can query … either directly or through your edge provider" [16].')} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('The difference is who holds the public key. In Web Bot Auth it\'s the agent\'s own website. Here it\'s the card network\'s registry, so a valid signature tells you the agent is enrolled in a card program [1, 15, 16]. Mastercard warns that agents people run on their own won\'t carry these signatures:')} />
    <PullQuote
      quote="Consumer-owned agents will not have scheme credentials — and that is expected."
      who="Mastercard"
      source="Agentic commerce guide"
      href={MASTERCARD}
    />
  </Band>

  <Band id="person" label="The person behind the agent" title="The person behind the agent: Sign in with ChatGPT and World ID">
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('You\'d meet these if you let readers\' assistants sign in for them. Sign in with ChatGPT links a customer\'s ChatGPT identity to their account with you: your site verifies the identity token, "creates or links the appropriate merchant account, and starts its own session". It\'s open to select commercial partners, and "a ChatGPT identity is not purchase approval" [5].')} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('World ID tells you a person stands behind an agent, and how well World checked. Its iris-scanning Orb "provides high assurance of unique humanness"; a camera check gives a medium-assurance signal [24]. Your site gets a signed result and an account ID that differs from one group of sites to the next [6]. World says what it leaves out: "This does not prove the agent itself is human or grant it access to another service" [25].')} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" id="anonymous" label="Anonymous credentials" title="Anonymous credentials">
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('Web Bot Auth leaves one problem open: a hosted agent platform signs for all of its users, so its signature can\'t tell one user from the next. Cloudflare won\'t use Web Bot Auth to name each user, because that "would create an unacceptable privacy risk". With anonymous credentials, the AI platform vouches for its user, and a site can rate-limit that user without learning who it is [26]. Apple\'s Private Access Tokens do the same for devices: they "prove when HTTP requests are coming from legitimate devices without disclosing someone\'s identity" [27].')} />
    <p class="prose" set:html={h('The Web Bot Auth working group decided in May 2026 to work on anonymous approaches alongside identity [28].')} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="other" label="Other schemes" title="Other schemes you'll hear about">
    <ResourceTable label="Other ways to identify an agent" cols={['', 'What it proves', 'Where you\'d meet it', 'Matters now?']} rows={OTHER} labelAll />
  </Band>

  <Band id="fora" label="FORA" title="How FORA answers the four questions">
    <PointList items={FORA_ANSWERS} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="near" title="Questions">
    <FaqGrid items={FAQ} />
  </Band>

  <Band tone="light" id="about">
    <Callout kind="about" points={ABOUT_FORA} columns />
    <section class="sources" id="sources" aria-labelledby="sources-label">
      <h2 class="end-label" id="sources-label">Sources</h2>
      <details class="all-sources" id="all-sources">
        <summary>All sources</summary>
        <SourceList groups={SOURCES} compact numbered />
      </details>
      <p class="note">Sources are checked on the date of publication.</p>
    </section>
    <section class="more-in" aria-labelledby="more-label">
      <h2 class="end-label" id="more-label">More in Resources</h2>
      <LinkRow links={MORE} stacked />
    </section>
  </Band>

  <ClosingCta
    title={TAGLINE}
    text="See what an agent would be offered for one of your pages through a FORA marketplace, which any company can run, or read how FORA agents sign."
  >
    <a href="/publishers/" class="btn btn-primary">See your offers</a>
    <a href="/protocol/authentication/" class="btn btn-ghost">How FORA agents sign</a>
  </ClosingCta>
</LandingLayout>

<script is:inline>
  // The sources sit in a closed <details>: a citation marker, or a link to
  // #src-N, opens it before the browser scrolls to the entry. The same script
  // as the standards map's.
  (function () {
    var box = document.getElementById('all-sources');
    if (!box) return;
    function open() {
      if (/^#src-\d+$/.test(location.hash) && !box.open) {
        box.open = true;
        var el = document.getElementById(location.hash.slice(1));
        if (el) el.scrollIntoView();
      }
    }
    window.addEventListener('hashchange', open);
    // A second click on the same marker fires no hashchange.
    document.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
      var link = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('a[href^="#src-"]');
      if (link) box.open = true;
    });
    open();
  })();
</script>

<style>
  /* Prose between components. The same few lines as on the TollBit page. */
  .prose { color: var(--lp-text); line-height: 1.65; max-width: 44rem; }
  .prose + .prose { margin-top: 1rem; }
  :global(*) + .prose { margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .prose :global(a) { color: var(--lp-link); }
  .prose :global(b) { color: var(--lp-strong); font-weight: 600; }
  .prose :global(code) { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.875em; color: var(--lp-heading); }
  /* On phones a figure beside steps comes before them. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    :global(#oauth .split > div:last-child) { order: -1; }
  }

  .sources { margin-top: 4rem; scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
  .more-in { margin-top: 3rem; }
  .end-label { font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--lp-label); margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  .all-sources > summary { cursor: pointer; color: var(--lp-link); font-weight: 600; }
  .note { max-width: 52rem; margin-top: 1.25rem; color: var(--lp-muted); font-size: 0.9375rem; line-height: 1.6; }
</style>
